    {
      "file": "materials/grammar_intro-simple-present-of-be-am-is-are.html",
      "type": "grammar_intro",
      "title": "Simple Present of 'be' — am, is, are",
      "competenceIds": [
        "fk.g.sentence_forms",
        "fk.g.simple_present",
        "fk.g.numbers",
        "fk.w.introduce_self",
        "fk.w.personal_questions",
        "fk.k.hoer.1",
        "fk.k.sprechen.1"
      ],
      "depth": "introduced",
      "contentText": [
        "In English, the verb \"be\" changes depending on who you talk about. Use \"am\" with \"I\", \"are\" with \"you\", and \"is\" with \"he\", \"she\", or a name.\n\nGood news: just like in German (\"Bist du?\"), English questions swap the verb and the subject — \"Are you?\" It works the same way!\n\nTo say something is not true, add \"not\" after the verb: \"I am not from France.\"\n\nOne important difference from German: in English, only names of people and places start with a capital letter. The word \"name\" stays lowercase: \"My name is Anna.\" not \"My Name is Anna.\"",
        "I am ten years old.",
        "You are Anna.",
        "He is Max.",
        "Are you Anna?",
        "I am not from France."
      ],
      "createdAt": "2026-07-30T10:15:30.796Z"
    },
    {
      "file": "materials/reading_text-three-new-friends.html",
